# Stage0 digests, COMMITTED deliberately — see docs/release/bootstrap.md §1.
# Pinned stage0 version: 0.1.5. Changing stage0 means changing this file,
# which means a reviewable commit rather than a server-side change.
#
# Moved 0.1.4 -> 0.1.5 in #1895, because the differentials could not go green
# against 0.1.4 and no change to them would have fixed it. Four behaviour
# changes landed after 0.1.4 was cut, and the oracle correctly does not know any
# of them:
#
#   - #1882 (fae789c) added E0073 in compiler/validateinst.bit, fired per
#     monomorphization. The 0.1.4 oracle ACCEPTS tests/cases/check_interp_generic.bit,
#     which this tree correctly REJECTS. selfhost-diffcheck.sh classifies "we
#     reject, oracle accepts" as FALSEPOS and treats it as fatal — rightly, since
#     a new diagnostic IS a behaviour change versus the last release. That is the
#     single reason the check differential could not reach FALSEPOS=0.
#   - #1903 (ac3f07c) const-expression array lengths, #1898 (f694acf) `T(x)` user
#     conversions, and #1914 struct-literal zero-init each made both IR
#     differentials diverge on two files apiece, for the same reason.
#
# None of the four is a regression, so softening a gate to absorb them would
# have written down a known difference instead of fixing it — exactly what #1883
# deleted the expected-mismatch lists to prevent.
#
# 0.1.5 was built by 0.1.4 in the normal way. The #1857 two-pass bootstrap via
# `BIT_STAGE0_BIN` was NOT needed and stays unused: nothing here depended on a
# fix that existed only downstream of the pinned compiler.
#
# Verified before pinning, on aarch64-macOS:
#   - all three artifacts downloaded ANONYMOUSLY (no token) and matched these
#     digests. An authenticated fetch proves nothing: a draft release answers a
#     token-bearing request and 404s an anonymous one.
#   - each artifact was smoke-tested UNPACKED by compiling and running a program
#     on hardware matching its triple, and asked for its own `--version`
#     (v0.1.0 shipped saying `0.1.0-dev` because nothing ever asked).
#   - x86_64-linux smoke-tested on REAL hardware, not emulation
#   - tag v0.1.5 lands on 82f74cd, the commit that was tested (#1856: `gh release
#     create` tags the REMOTE head, so main is pushed BEFORE the release is cut)
#
# THESE DIGESTS ARE NOT REPRODUCIBLE FROM SOURCE, and that is a known defect
# rather than a property of the artifact (#2060). dist/package.sh puts
# `--mtime=@0` and `--sort=name` in its GNU-tar branch only, and releases are cut
# on macOS, whose bsdtar supports neither — so every member carries the mtime of
# the `cp` that staged it and two builds of the same commit differ. Measured:
# cutting 0.1.5 twice, minutes apart, produced six different digests for three
# files. Verify a download against this file; do not expect a local rebuild to
# match it until #2060 lands.
#
# Compare bootstrap stages built to the SAME basename in DIFFERENT directories.
# A Mach-O ad-hoc signature embeds the binary's own file name, so `-o /tmp/stage2`
# vs `-o /tmp/stage3` differ at one byte range for that reason alone and look
# like a broken fixed point.
#
# EXACTLY ONE LINE PER TRIPLE. dist/stage0-verify.sh matches on the triple rather
# than the full filename (the version moves, the triple does not) and refuses
# outright when a triple has two digests. So the previous release's digests are
# REPLACED here, not appended — §3's diverse-double-compiling check gets the
# N-1 artifact from the releases page, not from this file.
